<resources>
    <!--
      setu - Sanskrit for "bridge", and the argument in one word: people become phones,
      phones become a network, the network becomes rescue infrastructure. Each step
      bridges to the next.

      Lower case on purpose, wherever it appears. An all-caps name shouting from the top
      of a screen whose job is to stay calm during an emergency is the wrong first
      impression - and this app already has exactly one thing allowed to shout, which is
      the mesh state banner.

      The macron on the e marks the long vowel of the original word - SAY-tu, not SET-oo.
      It is part of the name, not decoration.
    -->
    <!--
      Written as the XML escape rather than the literal character on purpose. The file is
      UTF-8 and the literal would work today, but a single tool re-saving this file in
      another encoding would turn the wordmark into a mojibake box on the splash screen -
      and nothing in the build would fail to warn about it. The escape is plain ASCII on
      disk and cannot break that way. It renders as e-macron.

      Checked: the bundled Noto Serif wordmark face has a real glyph for U+0113, so this
      is not a fallback-font composite.
    -->
    <string name="app_name">s&#x0113;tu</string>

    <!-- Held separately so the gloss can be shown or dropped without touching the wordmark. -->
    <string name="app_meaning">Sanskrit: bridge</string>
</resources>
